 ],
 "overview": "This file comprises records from State Department decimal file 894.24, covering Japanese army equipment and war supplies between 1932 and 1938. The bundle primarily consists of sixteen pages of cumulative docket sheets ('List of Papers') indexing sub-numbers 41 through 179, followed by an internal Division of Latin-American Affairs memorandum dated March 8, 1932, from H. Freeman Matthews to Assistant Secretary of State Harvey H. Bundy. The docket sheets record extensive intelligence collected by American diplomatic missions, consulates, and military and naval attachés detailing Japanese efforts to acquire strategic raw materials and military supplies—notably scrap iron, aviation gasoline, crude oil, copper, lead, nitrates, aircraft, and machine guns—from sources across the United States, Latin America, Europe, and Asia. The entries also trace mounting American public and congressional pressure to embargo military exports to Japan and the State Department's ongoing responses regarding the lack of statutory authority to restrict private trade under existing laws.",
